<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch10_P04_Ex2_Find_the_error</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the argument from the exercise above, in numbered lines.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>\(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta \equiv 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>\(\cos^2\theta \equiv 1 - \sin^2\theta\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>\(\cos\theta \equiv \sqrt{1 - \sin^2\theta}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\(1 + \cos\theta \equiv 1 + \sqrt{1 - \sin^2\theta}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Line 4 holds for every \(\theta\), so substitute \(\theta = \pi\): \(1 + \cos\pi = 1 + \sqrt{1 - \sin^2\pi}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(1 + (-1) = 1 + \sqrt{1 - 0^2}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>\(0 = 2\)</td></tr>
</table>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Which is the first line that does not follow from the lines before it?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Why not? (Optional: this box is not marked and not saved.)</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div></li>
<li><p>Give an example of a value of \(\theta\), in radians, with \(0 \leq \theta \leq 2\pi\), for which one of lines 1 to 4 is false. Type <code>pi</code> for \(\pi\), for example <code>pi/6</code>.</p>
<div>\(\theta =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Lines 1 and 2 are correct: line 1 is the Pythagorean identity, and line 2 subtracts \(\sin^2\theta\) from both sides.</p>
<p>Line 3 takes square roots, and that is where the argument fails. A square root is never negative: \(\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\), not \(x\). So what follows from line 2 is \(|\cos\theta| \equiv \sqrt{1 - \sin^2\theta}\). Line 3 drops the modulus, and it is false wherever \(\cos\theta \lt 0\), that is for \(\dfrac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \lt \dfrac{3\pi}{2}\), including \(\theta = \pi\). For example, at \(\theta = \dfrac{2\pi}{3}\) the left-hand side of line 3 is \(-\dfrac{1}{2}\) and the right-hand side is \(\dfrac{1}{2}\).</p>
<p>Lines 4 to 7 then follow correctly: substituting a value into an identity is allowed, and the arithmetic in line 6 is right. So the false conclusion \(0 = 2\) comes from line 3. With the modulus, \(\theta = \pi\) gives \(1 + |\cos\pi| = 2 = 1 + \sqrt{1}\), and there is no contradiction.</p>
<p>The same care is needed when solving equations: \(x^2 = 9\) gives \(x = 3\) or \(x = -3\).</p>
<p>A reason you might have written: <q>Line 3: the square root of \(\cos^2\theta\) is \(|\cos\theta|\), not \(\cos\theta\), and \(\cos\theta\) can be negative.</q></p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
